<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Friedrichstadt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1621">1621</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Friedrichstadt is a small town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig-holstein">Schleswig-Holstein</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, founded in 1621 by Duke Frederick III of Holstein-Gottorp for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/remonstrants">Remonstrants</a> fleeing persecution. Built in Dutch style with canals and gabled houses, it was meant to become a trading centre and offered freedom of worship to several faiths. It is still known as the Dutch town of the region.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/friedrichstadt.jpg" alt="Friedrichstadt" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Roger Veringmeier · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Friedrichstadt_Holl%C3%A4nderh%C3%A4user_Sleeswijk-Holstein_Duitsland.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Friedrichstadt was founded in 1621 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> settlers. Duke Friedrich III of Holstein-Gottorp persuaded them to bring capital and knowledge to the region in return for freedom to practise their Mennonite and Remonstrant faiths, and for the chance to reclaim fen and marsh land around the town. One of the settlers was Johannes Narssius. Dutch became an official language, and the town took its name from the duke.</p><p>By 1630 many of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/remonstrants">Arminians</a> had already returned to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>. Between 1633 and 1637 the duke sent an embassy to Tsar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-of-russia">Michael I of Russia</a> and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safi-of-persia">Shah Safi</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, hoping to make Friedrichstadt a European trade terminus. The delegation was led by the jurist Philip Crusius and the merchant Otto Brugman; their secretary, the scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adam-olearius">Adam Olearius</a>, later wrote a book about the journey. The aim of a regular trade route that avoided the passage round <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> was not achieved, and the town did not prosper as expected.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The town lies in the district of Nordfriesland, on the river Eider, about 12 km south of Husum. Besides <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/remonstrants">Remonstrants</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mennonites">Mennonites</a>, Unitarians, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quakers">Quakers</a>, Catholics and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> also settled there. Natives include Johannes Thedens, governor-general of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a> in 1741–1743, and the folklorist Wilhelm Mannhardt. The painter Jürgen Ovens, a pupil of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rembrandt">Rembrandt</a>, lived in the town and is buried in St. Christophorus Church.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Friedrichstadt” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The dukes kept a splendid Lutheran court at Gottorp, where Duke Frederick III founded <b>Friedrichstadt</b> for Dutch Remonstrants and sponsored Adam Olearius's embassy to Persia and the great Gottorp globe.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-holstein-gottorp">Duchy of Holstein-Gottorp</a></li></ul></section>
